DSL means Domainn Specific Language. A DSL is a small Language created inside the Kotlin to perform a specific function. It looks almost like a separate language. DSLs are useful especially for complicated data structures, hierarchical/nested structures and large amounts of configuration/data. They can remove repetitive boilerplate code. 
The most important feature used to create a Kotlin DSL is Function type with a receiver.  It allows us to write a lambda where an object becomes the implicit `this`.
Syntax:
ReceiverType.(ParameterTypes) > ReturnType
There is no special lambda syntax. Just give the lambda a function type with receiver.
Eg:
val myPlus3: String.(String) > String = { other >
    this + other
}
Syntax:
fun functionName(
    init: ClassName.() > Unit
) {
    ...
}

This `ClassName.() > Unit` is the core of a simple DSL. 
Kotlin's `apply` is very useful for DSL builders.
A DSL can contain nested DSLs. This is called a multilevel/hierarchical DSL. 
There is a safety problem with nested DSLs.  Kotlin could allow `title` from the outer `Dialog` to be accessed.  instead of something belonging to the button.
To solve this problem, Kotlin provides:
@DslMarker
We normally create our own annotation.Then annotate the DSL classes/functions. Now Kotlin prevents accidental access to outer receivers from inside nested DSL levels.
This is the basic pattern to remember:
@DslMarker
annotation class MyDsl
@MyDsl
class Builder {
    var name = ""
    fun child(init: Child.() > Unit) {
        // create child
    }
}
@MyDsl
class Child {
    var value = ""
}
fun build(init: Builder.() > Unit): Builder {
    return Builder().apply(init)
}
We should use a DSL when you need to represent:
1. complicated data
2. hierarchical/nested data
3. lots of configuration/data
4. repeated boilerplate structures
Do not use a DSL just because you can because it will make the code harder to understand, harder to debug, more difficult to create and will be a overkill for simple problems.
We use a DSL when simpler Kotlin features are not enough. 